A model \(m\) is deterministically accurate of order \(q\in\{1,2\}\) on \(B(x;\Delta)\) when fixed constants \(\kappa_j>0\) satisfy \[\|D^jf(y)-D^jm(y)\|\leq\kappa_j\Delta^{q+1-j}
\quad\text{for every }y\in B(x;\Delta),\ j=0,
\ldots,q.\] The assertion is deterministic: once \(f,m,x,\Delta\) are fixed, every displayed inequality must hold, with no exceptional event. A family is uniformly deterministically accurate when the same constants work for all admissible centers and radii.

Let \(D\) be nonsingular and consider \[\min_{p\in\mathbb R^n} f_k+
abla f_k^Tp+	frac12p^TB_kp\quad	ext{subject to}\quad\|Dp\|\leq\Delta_k.\] With \(\hat p=Dp\), \(\hat g_k=D^{-T}
abla f_k\), and, in the Newton model \(B_k=
abla^2f_k\), \(\hat B_k=D^{-T}
abla^2f_kD^{-1}\), the problem becomes \[\min_{\hat p\in\mathbb R^n} f_k+\hat g_k^T\hat p+	frac12\hat p^T\hat B_k\hat p\quad	ext{subject to}\quad\|\hat p\|\leq\Delta_k.\] Thus applying the ordinary trust-region Newton--CG algorithm in transformed variables is equivalent to applying its preconditioned variant to the weighted-norm problem; the latter's iterates grow monotonically in \(\|D\,\cdot\|\). For positive-definite \(B\), an incomplete Cholesky approximation may be written \(B=LL^T-R\) with restricted fill. If the Hessian is indefinite, an additional modification is required to handle the indefiniteness while preserving sparsity.
